[["heading","SECTION 23 / COMPARISON\nStateful vs Stateless authentication"],["sinhala","","Session-based සහ token-based approaches Jදකම secure කරන්න පුළුවන්. Architecture එක සහ",487],["sinhala","","logout/revocation requirements අනුව Jතහෝරන්න.",242],["heading","Session ID\nShared store lookup"],["heading","JWT\nLocal signature and claim checks"],["heading","Aspect\nStateful session\nStateless access token"],["text","Credential\nOpaque ID\nSigned self-contained token"],["text","Validation\nServer-side lookup\nSignature and claim checks"],["text","Logout\nDestroy session\nClear client token; revoke if supported"],["text","Revocation\nImmediate when store consulted\nExpiry or additional server state"],["text","Scaling\nShared session store or routing strategy\nDistributed key and policy management"],["text","Browser risks\nCookie CSRF; XSS actions\nDepends on storage and transport"],["heading","Interview answer"],["text","Stateful authentication maintains session state on the server. Stateless authentication validates\nself-contained credentials without a session lookup on each request. Both can scale; the tradeoff is\nrevocation, storage and operational complexity."]]